WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.862
This query left joins students to grades and groups by student.

00:00:03.862 --> 00:00:08.862
Filtered MAX expressions place each course grade in its own column.

00:00:08.862 --> 00:00:14.907
MAX works because the exercise expects at most one grade per student-course.

00:00:14.907 --> 00:00:19.087
Run the query and inspect how missing courses remain NULL.

00:00:19.087 --> 00:00:25.817
Connect this idea to a machine-learning workflow converting wide measurements into tidy long data.

00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:31.352
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:31.352 --> 00:00:39.202
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:39.202 --> 00:00:45.267
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:45.267 --> 00:00:51.440
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
